{"name":"Atrium White","hex":"#f1eee4","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"atrium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A central room or space in ancient Roman homes, open to the sky in the middle; a similar space in other buildings.","A square hall lit by daylight from above, into which rooms open at one or more levels."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atrium"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atrium (heart)","blocks":[{"text":"The atrium is one of the two upper chambers in the heart that receives blood from the circulatory system. The blood in the atria is pumped into the heart ventricles through the atrioventricular mitral and tricuspid heart valves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28cropped%29.svg/330px-Diagram_of_the_human_heart_%28cropped%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atrium_(heart)","wikidata":"Q1074839"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Heart_right_anatomy.jpg/500px-Heart_right_anatomy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Heart_right_anatomy.jpg/1280px-Heart_right_anatomy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Heart_right_anatomy.jpg/500px-Heart_right_anatomy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Right heart anatomy, right ventricle seen on right of illustration","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeart_right_anatomy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Heart_left_atrial_appendage_tee_view.jpg/500px-Heart_left_atrial_appendage_tee_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Heart_left_atrial_appendage_tee_view.jpg/500px-Heart_left_atrial_appendage_tee_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Left atrial appendage shown at upper right","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeart_left_atrial_appendage_tee_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Thrombus_linkes_Herzohr_92jw_-_CT_axial_und_coronar_-_001.jpg/500px-Thrombus_linkes_Herzohr_92jw_-_CT_axial_und_coronar_-_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Thrombus_linkes_Herzohr_92jw_-_CT_axial_und_coronar_-_001.jpg/1280px-Thrombus_linkes_Herzohr_92jw_-_CT_axial_und_coronar_-_001.jpg?